<article class="env-remark" id="8F74DA">
  <p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/8F74DA">Remark <span data-tag="8F74DA">3.1.4.6</span>.</a> Suppose $S$ is a Kan complex. A map $p: X \rightarrow S$ is a Cartesian fibration if and only if it is a coCartesian fibration (this follows in general from Proposition <a href="/tag/925E10" data-tag="925E10">3.3.1.8</a>; if $S = \Delta ^0$, the main case of interest for us, it is obvious). Moreover, the class $p$-coCartesian edges of $X$ coincides with the class of $p$-Cartesian edges of $X$: both may be described as the class of equivalences in $X$. Consequently, if $A \in (\mSet )_{/S}$, then </p>
  <div class="equation">
  \[  \bHom ^{\flat }_{S}(A, X^{\natural }) \simeq \bHom ^{\flat }_{S^{op}}(A^{op}, (X^{op})^{\natural })^{op}, \]
</div>
  <p> where $A^{op}$ is regarded as a marked simplicial set in the obvious way. It follows that a map $A \rightarrow B$ is a Cartesian equivalence in $(\mSet )_{/S}$ if and only if $A^{op} \rightarrow B^{op}$ is a Cartesian equivalence in $(\mSet )_{/S^{op}}$. In other words, the Cartesian model structure on $(\mSet )_{/S}$ is self-dual <em>when $S$ is a Kan complex</em>. In particular, if $S = \Delta ^0$, we deduce that the functor </p>
  <div class="equation">
  \[  A \mapsto A^{op} \]
</div>
  <p> determines an autoequivalence of the model category $\mSet \simeq (\mSet )_{/\Delta ^0}$. </p>
</article>